TO the Editor of The National Republican;

SIR—We wish that the wise and influential heads of Washington would once in a while walk up around the south side of the Capitol and appoint a committee of the more active ones among them to ascend the highest lookout of the dome and survey the scene to the south. It is by far the most repulsive and even unhealthy spot of the whole city, and it seems strange that so repulsive a sight and one so inviting to disease will year after year be allowed to remain not materially unchanged, and no one of authority heed it or take active measures to remedy so grave an evil. Sure the old cause has been partly filled in, but then that is not one-tenth that ought to be done. The filling-in of the canal should be completed. Then the Government should condemn all the land immediately south of the Capitol and have the same uniformly filled in and graded, and all the loose material of Government put into some better and less conspicuous storage place, and all the vile rubbish which now is allowed to accumulate there be better provided for. We trust that this great eyesore and disease-breeding spot may ere long receive the careful attention of the Engineer and landscape gardener. Then this view from the Capitol will be beautiful and attractive, and, in a sanitary point of view, the whole city will feel its beneficial effects. This certainly is a point well worth agitating, and we trust that it will be agitated until so grave a nuisance and repulsive feature has been changed into one that is beautiful and healthy.

PHILO.
